      "detail": "REGISTRY CORRECTION: the CivicClerk OData route given as https://mobileal.portal.civicclerk.com/api/v1/Events returns HTTP 404 (ASP.NET 'resource has been removed'). The working host is https://mobileal.api.civicclerk.com/v1/Events (HTTP 200, OData JSON). However $orderby/$filter/$select query options on that endpoint also 404 - only the bare collection responds. Rung 6 was not needed because a better source was found."
    },
    {
      "step": "cycle_calendar",
      "rung": null,
      "outcome": "success",
      "tool_calls": 3,
      "searches": 1,
      "detail": "Mobile confirmed exempt from the 2021 Alabama act: odd-year cycle 2017-08-22, 2021-08-24 (runoff 2021-10-05), 2025-08-26 (mayoral runoff 2025-09-23). Plus one off-cycle special: 2023-07-25 for District 6. Terms begin the first Monday in November; FY2025 ACFR org chart is dated 'as of 11/03/2025', confirming the 2025 seating date, and 'as of 09/30/2025' for the outgoing body."
    },
    {
      "step": "cycle_retrieval",
      "rung": 4,
      "outcome": "success",
      "tool_calls": 4,
      "searches": 1,
      "detail": "2017 and 2021 district-by-district results retrieved verbatim from Bama Politics constructible URLs (/alabama/alabama-elections/{YEAR}-alabama-elections-results/{YEAR}-mobile-al-city-council-district-{N}-election/), all 7 districts each year, including the Oct 5 2021 runoffs in D1, D2 and D6. 2025 results taken from the Wikipedia article's raw wikitext, which cites the city's own official PDF (cityofmobile.org/uploads/250902031509OFFICIALELECTIONRESULTS2025001.pdf). That PDF downloads fine but is a SCANNED IMAGE - pdftotext yields 6 bytes; it would need OCR."
    },
    {
      "step": "escalation",
      "rung": 7,
      "outcome": "success",
      "tool_calls": 4,
      "searches": 1,
      "detail": "HIGHEST-VALUE SOURCE FOR THIS CITY: the City of Mobile's own CAFR/ACFR series at https://www.cityofmobile.gov/government/financials/ carries an organizational chart naming every council member by district plus the mayor, one per fiscal year. Verified FY2019, FY2020, FY2021, FY2022, FY2023, FY2024, FY2025 - all fetch by curl and extract cleanly with pdftotext -layout. FY2022 onward also annotate '(President)' and '(Vice President)'. The FY2025 ACFR uniquely contains TWO charts, 'as of 09/30/2025' and 'as of 11/03/2025', so it alone documents the entire 2025 handover. This is a better Alabama route than the EOPA audit reports for a city this size - EOPA has Mobile COUNTY reports but no City of Mobile municipal audit was found."
    },
    {
      "step": "midterm_check",
      "rung": null,
      "outcome": "success",
      "tool_calls": 5,
      "searches": 4,
      "detail": "TWO mid-term changes found, both invisible in election results. (1) District 2: Council President Rev. Levon C. Manzie DIED in office on 2021-09-19 at age 38, sixteen days before the Oct 5 2021 runoff in his own district. Alabama law required the runoff to proceed with his name on the ballot; he 'received' 1,100 votes posthumously and William Carroll won with 1,464. D2 and the Council Presidency were vacant from 2021-09-19 until the new council was seated 2021-11-01. (2) District 6: Scott Jones resigned in April 2023 over the council's continued support of AltaPointe and its CEO; the council set a special election for 2023-07-25, Josh Woods won it with ~54%, and was sworn in 2023-08-08 after what WKRG called a 'four-month vacancy'. Explicit searches for 2019/2020 appointments and vacancies returned nothing, and the FY2019 and FY2020 ACFR org charts list the identical seven 2017-elected members, so the 2017-2021 term appears to have had no other change."
    },
    {
      "step": "validation",
      "rung": null,
      "outcome": "success",
